# KAMPYN — Git Rules

## 1. Purpose

These rules define how Git should be used across KAMPYN.

The goal is to maintain a repository history that is:

- understandable
- reviewable
- traceable
- recoverable
- safe
- easy to maintain
- suitable for collaborative development

Git history is part of the project's engineering record.

Do not treat commits as temporary containers for unrelated work.

General engineering rules are defined in `AGENTS.md`.

---

# 2. Core Git Principles

Prefer:

```text
small logical changes
focused commits
descriptive messages
reviewable pull requests
clean history
traceable changes
safe operations
```

Avoid:

```text
giant commits
unrelated changes
meaningless commit messages
force-pushing shared history
committing secrets
mixing refactors with features
```

Git should preserve the reasoning and evolution of the system.

---

# 3. Inspect Before Git Operations

Before performing significant Git operations, inspect the repository state.

Use the equivalent of:

```bash
git status
git branch
git log
```

as appropriate.

Understand:

```text
current branch
working tree changes
staged changes
untracked files
recent commits
upstream relationship
```

Do not assume the working tree is clean.

Never overwrite existing user changes without explicit authorization.

---

# 4. Protect Existing Work

User changes are authoritative.

Before modifying files:

```text
inspect current changes
understand what is already modified
avoid touching unrelated work
```

If the working tree contains changes unrelated to the current task:

- leave them untouched
- avoid staging them
- do not reset them
- do not stash them automatically
- do not overwrite them

Do not use destructive commands simply to obtain a clean working tree.

---

# 5. Never Destroy Uncommitted Work

Do not execute destructive Git operations without explicit authorization.

Examples include:

```bash
git reset --hard
git clean -fd
git checkout -- .
git restore .
git stash drop
```

or equivalent operations that can remove user work.

If destructive cleanup appears necessary, explain the risk and obtain explicit authorization first.

---

# 6. Branching

Use branches according to the repository's established branching strategy.

Branch names should communicate purpose.

Prefer patterns such as:

```text
feature/<short-description>
fix/<short-description>
refactor/<short-description>
docs/<short-description>
test/<short-description>
chore/<short-description>
security/<short-description>
```

Follow the project's existing naming convention when one already exists.

Do not create unnecessary branches for trivial local work if the repository workflow does not require them.

---

# 7. Branch Scope

A branch should represent one coherent unit of work.

Avoid combining unrelated work such as:

```text
feature implementation
dependency upgrade
unrelated refactor
documentation cleanup
formatting entire repository
```

in the same branch unless the changes are genuinely related.

Focused branches make review and rollback easier.

---

# 8. Branch Naming

Branch names should be:

- short
- descriptive
- lowercase where the repository convention permits
- free of unnecessary punctuation
- easy to identify in CI and pull requests

Prefer:

```text
feature/order-tracking
fix/payment-webhook-retry
refactor/search-adapter
security/session-validation
```

over:

```text
my-branch
changes
test123
new-feature-final
ansh-work-2
```

---

# 9. Commits

Each commit should represent one logical change.

A good commit should ideally be:

```text
coherent
buildable
understandable
reviewable
```

Avoid commits that mix unrelated modifications.

For example, do not combine:

```text
implement booking API
rename unrelated frontend components
update deployment configuration
```

unless they are required parts of the same change.

---

# 10. Commit Messages

Commit messages should explain the change.

Prefer:

```text
feat: add booking availability endpoint
fix: prevent duplicate payment processing
refactor: isolate search indexing adapter
docs: document self-hosted deployment
test: add tenant isolation coverage
chore: update Go dependencies
```

Avoid:

```text
update
changes
fix
stuff
final
done
work
misc
```

Follow the repository's established commit convention when one exists.

If Conventional Commits are used, maintain consistency with them.

---

# 11. Commit Message Structure

When using Conventional Commits:

```text
<type>: <short description>
```

Common types include:

```text
feat
fix
refactor
test
docs
chore
perf
security
build
ci
```

Use a scope only when the repository convention benefits from it:

```text
feat(orders): add cancellation flow
fix(auth): reject expired refresh tokens
```

Do not force scopes everywhere if they add noise.

---

# 12. Commit Granularity

Prefer commits that can be understood independently.

Good:

```text
1. Add order cancellation domain rule
2. Add repository support for cancellation
3. Add API endpoint
4. Add integration tests
5. Document API behavior
```

when the project workflow benefits from such separation.

Do not split a naturally atomic change into dozens of meaningless commits.

The objective is logical history, not maximum commit count.

---

# 13. Formatting-Only Changes

Do not mix broad formatting changes with functional work.

Avoid commits containing:

```text
feature implementation
+
entire repository formatting
+
unrelated import reordering
```

unless formatting is required by the change.

Large formatting changes make review and debugging significantly harder.

---

# 14. Refactoring

Keep refactoring separate from behavior changes when practical.

Prefer:

```text
refactor existing architecture
        ↓
verify behavior remains unchanged
        ↓
implement new behavior
```

rather than:

```text
massive refactor
+
new feature
+
dependency upgrade
+
unrelated fixes
```

This makes regressions easier to identify.

---

# 15. Dependency Changes

Dependency upgrades should be intentional.

Do not bundle unrelated dependency changes into feature commits.

When upgrading a dependency:

1. Understand why it is changing.
2. Review compatibility implications.
3. Update the appropriate manifest and lockfile.
4. Run relevant tests.
5. Review the resulting diff.

Security-driven dependency upgrades may reasonably be isolated into dedicated changes.

---

# 16. Lockfiles

Lockfiles are part of the dependency state.

When dependency manifests change:

- update the appropriate lockfile
- review unexpected dependency changes
- do not manually edit generated lockfile sections unless required
- commit lockfile changes when the repository tracks them

Do not delete lockfiles merely to resolve dependency problems.

---

# 17. Generated Files

Follow the repository's policy for generated files.

Before committing generated output, determine whether it is:

```text
tracked source
generated artifact
build output
temporary output
```

Do not commit build artifacts unless the project explicitly requires them.

When generated files are intentionally tracked, regenerate them using the project's official tooling.

---

# 18. Secrets

Never commit secrets.

Check for:

```text
API keys
passwords
tokens
private keys
cloud credentials
database credentials
OAuth secrets
webhook secrets
```

before committing.

Sensitive files such as:

```text
.env
.env.local
credentials.json
private keys
```

must follow the repository's ignore and secret-management policy.

---

# 19. If a Secret Is Committed

Treat accidental secret commits as security incidents.

Do not assume that deleting the file in a later commit removes the secret from repository history.

If a secret was committed:

1. Stop further propagation.
2. Identify the exposed credential.
3. Rotate or revoke it.
4. Follow the project's security incident process.
5. Determine whether history rewriting is required.
6. Remove the secret according to the repository's approved procedure.

Do not attempt history rewriting casually on shared branches.

---

# 20. Staging

Stage intentionally.

Before committing, inspect:

```bash
git diff
git diff --staged
```

as appropriate.

Ensure the staged change contains only the intended work.

Do not blindly use:

```bash
git add .
```

when unrelated changes may exist.

Prefer explicitly staging relevant files or reviewing the staged diff carefully.

---

# 21. Commit Review

Before creating a commit, verify:

```text
what changed
why it changed
which files changed
whether unrelated files are included
whether secrets are present
whether generated files are expected
whether tests/documentation are included where required
```

The commit should tell a coherent story.

---

# 22. Pull Requests

Pull requests should be focused and explain the change clearly.

A useful PR description should communicate:

```text
what changed
why it changed
important implementation decisions
testing performed
known limitations
migration requirements
security considerations where relevant
```

Do not make reviewers reconstruct the purpose of a change from the diff alone.

---

# 23. Pull Request Size

Prefer reasonably sized pull requests.

If a change is large because the problem genuinely requires it, structure the implementation clearly.

Avoid adding unrelated cleanup simply because a large PR already exists.

Large changes should make logical boundaries visible through:

- commits
- directory structure
- documentation
- PR description

---

# 24. Reviewability

Before opening or updating a PR, inspect the final diff.

Look for:

```text
unrelated changes
debugging code
temporary code
secrets
unexpected generated files
accidental deletions
API changes
database changes
configuration changes
dependency changes
```

Do not assume a successful build means the diff is correct.

---

# 25. Pull Request Dependencies

If one PR depends on another:

- make the dependency explicit
- document the expected merge order
- keep the dependent changes focused

Avoid creating hidden dependencies between unrelated branches.

---

# 26. Updating a Branch

When bringing a branch up to date, follow the repository's established workflow.

Possible strategies include:

```text
merge
rebase
```

Choose based on the project's collaboration and history policy.

Do not rewrite shared history merely to make the graph look cleaner.

---

# 27. Rebase

Rebase is appropriate when rewriting history is safe and useful.

Before rebasing:

```text
confirm the branch is not shared in a way that makes rewriting harmful
understand which commits will be rewritten
ensure important work is recoverable
```

Do not casually rebase shared branches.

Never rebase a protected or shared branch against repository policy.

---

# 28. Force Push

Force-pushing can destroy shared history.

Avoid:

```bash
git push --force
```

Prefer:

```bash
git push --force-with-lease
```

only when history rewriting is intentional, authorized, and safe.

Never force-push protected branches unless the repository explicitly permits it and the operation is authorized.

---

# 29. Merge Conflicts

When conflicts occur:

1. Understand both sides.
2. Identify the intended behavior.
3. Preserve required changes from both branches.
4. Resolve the conflict deliberately.
5. Run relevant tests.
6. Review the resulting diff.
7. Complete the merge or rebase.

Do not resolve conflicts by automatically choosing:

```text
ours
theirs
```

without understanding the semantic impact.

---

# 30. Conflict Resolution

A conflict is not merely a text problem.

Determine:

```text
Which behavior should exist?
Did either branch change an API?
Did either branch change a database contract?
Did either branch change security behavior?
Did either branch change architecture?
```

When uncertain, inspect the surrounding history and source before deciding.

Do not invent behavior to make a conflict disappear.

---

# 31. Merge Verification

After resolving conflicts, verify the resulting system.

At minimum, where applicable:

```text
typecheck
lint
tests
build
```

Also inspect:

```bash
git diff
git status
```

to ensure no accidental files or conflict markers remain.

---

# 32. Tags and Releases

Tags should represent meaningful repository states.

Use the project's established versioning strategy.

For releases, verify:

```text
version
changelog
migration requirements
build artifacts
dependency state
tests
deployment compatibility
```

Do not create release tags for arbitrary intermediate states unless the project explicitly uses them.

---

# 33. Versioning

If KAMPYN uses semantic versioning:

```text
MAJOR
breaking changes

MINOR
backward-compatible functionality

PATCH
backward-compatible fixes
```

Follow the actual release policy of the repository rather than assuming semantic versioning is in use.

Breaking API, SDK, configuration, or deployment changes must be identified clearly.

---

# 34. Reverts

Prefer reverting a problematic change rather than manually creating a second unrelated fix when reverting is the safest way to restore a known-good state.

A revert should:

- identify the behavior being reversed
- preserve history
- be reviewable
- be followed by appropriate tests

Do not rewrite history to hide a problematic change on shared branches.

---

# 35. Cherry-Picking

Cherry-pick only when there is a clear reason to move a specific commit between branches.

Before cherry-picking:

- understand the commit's dependencies
- inspect its parent changes
- check whether it relies on other commits
- verify the resulting state

Do not cherry-pick a commit blindly because its message sounds relevant.

---

# 36. Git Bisect and History Investigation

Use Git history when it provides evidence useful for understanding behavior.

Useful tools include:

```bash
git log
git show
git blame
git diff
git bisect
```

Use history to answer questions such as:

```text
When was this behavior introduced?
Why was this code written?
Which change caused this regression?
What contract existed previously?
```

Do not use historical code as authoritative merely because it existed previously.

Current architecture and requirements remain authoritative.

---

# 37. Blame Usage

`git blame` is an investigation tool, not a tool for assigning personal responsibility.

Use it to locate:

```text
relevant commit
historical context
ownership clues
```

Then inspect the corresponding commit and surrounding history.

Do not infer intent or fault from authorship metadata alone.

---

# 38. Working With Uncommitted Changes

If uncommitted changes exist:

- inspect them
- preserve them
- avoid staging unrelated files
- avoid resetting them
- avoid overwriting them

If the current task overlaps with existing modifications, determine whether they are part of the task before changing them.

When ambiguity materially affects safety, ask before proceeding.

---

# 39. Git and Automation

Automated agents must be conservative with repository operations.

An AI agent must not:

```text
reset user work
delete branches
rewrite shared history
force-push
delete tags
clean untracked files
modify unrelated commits
```

without explicit authorization.

AI-generated commits must follow the same standards as human-created commits.

---

# 40. Git Hooks and CI

Do not bypass repository safeguards merely to complete a task.

Avoid:

```bash
git commit --no-verify
```

or equivalent bypasses unless there is an explicit reason and authorization.

If a hook fails:

1. Understand the failure.
2. Fix the underlying issue when appropriate.
3. Re-run the hook.
4. Only bypass it when the project workflow explicitly permits doing so.

---

# 41. Monorepo Changes

KAMPYN may contain multiple applications, packages, services, SDKs, or infrastructure components.

Commit boundaries should reflect logical ownership.

A change spanning multiple packages is acceptable when they form one coherent feature or contract change.

Do not split a single atomic API contract change into unrelated commits merely because multiple packages are affected.

---

# 42. Cross-Repository Changes

When a change requires synchronized updates across repositories:

```text
document the dependency
identify compatible versions
update consumers/providers deliberately
verify compatibility
```

Do not silently assume that another repository will be updated later.

For SDK/API changes, clearly identify:

```text
producer version
consumer version
migration requirements
compatibility window
```

where applicable.

---

# 43. Database Migrations

Database migrations require special Git discipline.

A migration should:

- be versioned
- be ordered
- be reproducible
- correspond to the intended schema change
- avoid accidental destructive behavior
- be reviewed with the application change

Do not edit an already-applied migration casually.

If a migration has already been shared or deployed, follow the project's migration correction strategy rather than rewriting history.

---

# 44. Configuration Changes

Configuration changes should be visible in Git history.

When changing:

```text
environment variables
deployment configuration
Docker
Kubernetes
CI/CD
infrastructure
feature flags
```

include the relevant documentation and validation where appropriate.

Never commit production secrets as configuration values.

---

# 45. Git Hygiene

Keep the repository free of unnecessary artifacts.

Do not commit:

```text
build output
temporary files
editor files
local databases
logs
debug dumps
coverage output
OS metadata
personal configuration
```

unless the repository explicitly tracks them.

Keep `.gitignore` aligned with the actual project tooling.

---

# 46. Final Diff Review

Before considering Git work complete, inspect:

```bash
git status
git diff
git diff --staged
```

as appropriate.

Check specifically for:

- accidental files
- deleted files
- renamed files
- secrets
- unrelated formatting
- debug code
- generated artifacts
- unexpected dependency changes
- configuration changes
- migration changes

Do not rely solely on IDE source-control views.

---

# 47. Git Completion Checklist

Before considering a Git operation or change complete:

- [ ] Current branch was understood.
- [ ] Existing user changes were preserved.
- [ ] No destructive operation was performed without authorization.
- [ ] Changes are logically scoped.
- [ ] Commit messages clearly describe their changes.
- [ ] No secrets are committed.
- [ ] No unnecessary generated artifacts are committed.
- [ ] Relevant tests and checks were run.
- [ ] The final diff was reviewed.
- [ ] Conflicts were resolved intentionally.
- [ ] Shared history was not rewritten unnecessarily.
- [ ] Force-push was avoided unless explicitly justified.
- [ ] Documentation was updated where required.
- [ ] Database/configuration changes were reviewed.
- [ ] The resulting repository state is understandable and recoverable.

---

# 48. Final Principle

Git history should help an engineer understand how KAMPYN reached its current state.

Every change should be:

```text
intentional
traceable
reviewable
recoverable
```

> **Protect existing work.**  
> **Keep commits logical.**  
> **Keep branches focused.**  
> **Never commit secrets.**  
> **Do not rewrite shared history casually.**  
> **Resolve conflicts by understanding behavior, not by choosing a side blindly.**  
> **Review the final diff before declaring work complete.**  
> **Treat Git history as part of the engineering record.**
